The formula presents two central tenets of Bohmian mechanics. The first part, $\mathbf{v}_i =  rac{d\mathbf{Q}_i}{dt} =  rac{\hbar}{m_i} \operatorname{Im}\left( rac{
abla_i \psi}{\psi}ight)$, is the guidance equation for the $i$-th particle. Here, $\mathbf{v}_i$ is the velocity of the particle located at position $\mathbf{Q}_i$, $m_i$ is its mass, and $\hbar$ is the reduced Planck constant. The velocity is proportional to the imaginary part of the logarithmic derivative of the multi-particle wave function $\psi = \psi(\mathbf{Q}_1, ..., \mathbf{Q}_N, t)$. By expressing $\psi$ in polar form as $\psi = R e^{iS/\hbar}$, where $R$ is the real amplitude and $S$ is the real phase (action), the velocity simplifies to $\mathbf{v}_i =  rac{
abla_i S}{m_i}$. This establishes a direct link to the classical Hamilton-Jacobi formalism. The second part, $Q = -\sum_{i}  rac{\hbar^2}{2m_i}  rac{
abla_i^2 R}{R}$, defines the quantum potential $Q$. This term depends on the curvature (second spatial derivative) of the wave function's amplitude $R$. It is a holistic, non-local potential that depends on the configuration of all particles in the system simultaneously, thereby mediating quantum effects like entanglement and interference.
